{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-168073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T(t)$ represent the temperature of an object at time $t,$ in minutes, since the moment when it was put in a room. The temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times the difference between the object's temperature and the room's temperature, which is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWhich differential equation describes the instantaneous rate of change in the object's temperature?\n\n*Hint: Write the right-hand side of the equation as an expression in terms of $T.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rate of change in the temperature $T$ of the object is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe are told that the temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times $(T-25).$ Therefore, the corresponding differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-0.03(T-25).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "68688f4ebccaf973a3e9cdb8d472c42555655a76b483f5240a0ecdc4652bfb48"} {"question" "q-168073" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168073" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.03(T-25)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T(t)$ represent the temperature of an object at time $t,$ in minutes, since the moment when it was put in a room. The temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times the difference between the object's temperature and the room's temperature, which is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWhich differential equation describes the instantaneous rate of change in the object's temperature?\n\n*Hint: Write the right-hand side of the equation as an expression in terms of $T.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-0.03(T-25)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nHow fast is the object's temperature *decreasing* at the moment when its temperature is ${60}^{∘}C?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The object's temperature is decreasing at a rate of $-$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$degrees per minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the rate, we substitute $T=60$ into our equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}{|}_{T=60} & =-0.03(T-25){|}_{T=60} \\\\  & =-0.03(60-25) \\\\  & =-0.03(35) \\\\  & =-1.05\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $T={60}^{∘}C,$ the object's temperature decreases at a rate of $1.05$ degrees per minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "68688f4ebccaf973a3e9cdb8d472c42555655a76b483f5240a0ecdc4652bfb48"} {"question" "q-168074" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168074" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.05"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T(t)$ represent the temperature of an object at time $t,$ in minutes, since the moment when it was put in a room. The temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times the difference between the object's temperature and the room's temperature, which is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWhich differential equation describes the instantaneous rate of change in the object's temperature?\n\n*Hint: Write the right-hand side of the equation as an expression in terms of $T.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-0.03(T-25)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nHow fast is the object's temperature *decreasing* at the moment when its temperature is ${60}^{∘}C?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The object's temperature is decreasing at a rate of $-$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1.05$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$degrees per minute.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}$ in terms of $T.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-0.03(T-25)) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-0.03T+0.75) \\\\  & =-0.03\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(T)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0.75) \\\\  & =-0.03\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ into the above and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =-0.03\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =-0.03(-0.03(T-25)) \\\\  & =0.0009(T-25)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "68688f4ebccaf973a3e9cdb8d472c42555655a76b483f5240a0ecdc4652bfb48"} {"question" "q-168076" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168076" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.0009(T-25)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T(t)$ represent the temperature of an object at time $t,$ in minutes, since the moment when it was put in a room. The temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times the difference between the object's temperature and the room's temperature, which is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWhich differential equation describes the instantaneous rate of change in the object's temperature?\n\n*Hint: Write the right-hand side of the equation as an expression in terms of $T.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-0.03(T-25)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nHow fast is the object's temperature *decreasing* at the moment when its temperature is ${60}^{∘}C?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The object's temperature is decreasing at a rate of $-$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1.05$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$degrees per minute.\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}$ in terms of $T.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0.0009(T-25)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the general solution to the differential equation in part 1 for $T\\ne 25.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $T\\,=\\,25+K\\cdot \\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\,$where $K$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution, we separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03(T-25) \\\\ \\frac{1}{T-25}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{T-25}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int -0.03\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-25} & =-0.03\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.03t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a perfectly valid general solution. However, if we want to write $T=T(t)$ explicitly, then we can solve for $T.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.03t+C \\\\ |T-25| & ={e}^{-0.03t+C} \\\\ |T-25| & =K\\,{e}^{-0.03t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}$ is an arbitrary positive constant.\n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be both positive and negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =25+K\\,{e}^{-0.03t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168077" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "68688f4ebccaf973a3e9cdb8d472c42555655a76b483f5240a0ecdc4652bfb48"} {"question" "q-168077" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168077" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e^{-0.03t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T(t)$ represent the temperature of an object at time $t,$ in minutes, since the moment when it was put in a room. The temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times the difference between the object's temperature and the room's temperature, which is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWhich differential equation describes the instantaneous rate of change in the object's temperature?\n\n*Hint: Write the right-hand side of the equation as an expression in terms of $T.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-0.03(T-25)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nHow fast is the object's temperature *decreasing* at the moment when its temperature is ${60}^{∘}C?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The object's temperature is decreasing at a rate of $-$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1.05$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$degrees per minute.\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}$ in terms of $T.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0.0009(T-25)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFind the general solution to the differential equation in part 1 for $T\\ne 25.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $T\\,=\\,25+K\\cdot \\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^{-0.03t}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\,$where $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nLet $\\overset{^}{T}(t)$ be a particular solution of the differential equation from the previous part that satisfies the condition $\\overset{^}{T}(0)=28.$ Find $\\overset{^}{T}(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\overset{^}{T}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Previously, we found that the general solution is\n\n$$\nT=25+K{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $T(0)=28$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =25+K\\,{e}^{-0.03(0)} \\\\ 28 & =25+K\\,{e}^{0} \\\\ K & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the required particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{T}(t)=25+3{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "68688f4ebccaf973a3e9cdb8d472c42555655a76b483f5240a0ecdc4652bfb48"} {"question" "q-168078" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168078" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "25+3e^{-0.03t}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T(t)$ represent the temperature of an object at time $t,$ in minutes, since the moment when it was put in a room. The temperature *decreases* at a rate that is equal to $0.03$ times the difference between the object's temperature and the room's temperature, which is ${25}^{∘}\\text{C}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nWhich differential equation describes the instantaneous rate of change in the object's temperature?\n\n*Hint: Write the right-hand side of the equation as an expression in terms of $T.$*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-0.03(T-25)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nHow fast is the object's temperature *decreasing* at the moment when its temperature is ${60}^{∘}C?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The object's temperature is decreasing at a rate of $-$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1.05$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$degrees per minute.\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}$ in terms of $T.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0.0009(T-25)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFind the general solution to the differential equation in part 1 for $T\\ne 25.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $T\\,=\\,25+K\\cdot \\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^{-0.03t}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\,$where $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nLet $\\overset{^}{T}(t)$ be a particular solution of the differential equation from the previous part that satisfies the condition $\\overset{^}{T}(0)=28.$ Find $\\overset{^}{T}(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\overset{^}{T}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $25+3e^{-0.03t}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFor the function $\\overset{^}{T}(t)$ from the previous part, what is ${lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\overset{^}{T}(t)?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\overset{^}{T}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the previous part, we know that\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{T}(t)=25+3{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\overset{^}{T}(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(25+3{e}^{-0.03t}) \\\\  & =25+3{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }{e}^{-0.03t} \\\\  & =25+3\\,{e}^{\\,\\,{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(-0.03t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{\\,\\,{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(-0.03t)}=\\infty ,$ and the positive factor of $3$ does not affect the sign of the limit, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to -\\,\\infty }\\overset{^}{T}(t)=\\infty .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "68688f4ebccaf973a3e9cdb8d472c42555655a76b483f5240a0ecdc4652bfb48"} {"question" "q-168079" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"} {"question" "q-168079" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037045/state.json" "file_sha256" "287f4e5457d95c61b4ae1c9044f774ed18594527677b81644414b5635d7a8076"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1252 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
